  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+x)^{3}}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f(x).$\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$",
